{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And not with you alone do I cut this covenant, etc.</b> Most of the commentators found great difficulty with this oath that was imposed upon them and upon their seed who were not yet in the world — how could they enter the covenant of the oath? The root of this difficulty you will find in the Akeidah, and following his opinion also the Mahar[i'a?] and other commentators. And the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Tanchuma, Nitzavim 3) is that all the souls destined to be created were there. And some have deduced this from the change of language in the verse: ״כִּי אֶת אֲשֶׁר יֶשְׁנוּ פֹּה עִמָּנוּ עֹמֵד הַיּוֹם לִפְנֵי ה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ וְאֵת אֲשֶׁר אֵינֶנּוּ פֹּה עִמָּנוּ הַיּוֹם״, and it does not say ״וְאֵת אֲשֶׁר אֵינֶנּוּ פֹּה עִמָּנוּ עֹמֵד הַיּוֹם לִפְנֵי ה׳״ — hear from this that it is speaking of souls, that even though they are not here with us, nevertheless they stand before HaShem always beneath the Throne of Glory, like the angels who are called 'standing,' as it says (Zechariah 3:7): ״וְנָתַתִּי לְךָ מַהְלָכִים בֵּין הָעֹמְדִים הָאֵלֶּה״. It also appears that one may deduce this from the word ״עִמָּנוּ״, for it should have said ״וְאֵת אֲשֶׁר אֵינֶנּוּ פֹּה הַיּוֹם״ — but the precision of this is that specifically 'with us' they are not, but they exist with HaShem and stand before Him. And as for what they objected: can one really impose an oath upon a soul? — we find a precedent for this that our Rabbis of blessed memory said in tractate Niddah (30a), that on the day of birth they impose an oath upon the person: be righteous and do not be wicked, etc." "And it appears that the entire thrust of the verses speaks of this matter, for one may ask concerning what it says, ״כִּי אַתֶּם יְדַעְתֶּם אֵת אֲשֶׁר יָשַׁבְנוּ״ etc., and likewise the two occurrences of ״פֶּן״ — why do I need them? ״פֶּן יֵשׁ בָּכֶם אִישׁ״, ״פֶּן יֵשׁ בָּכֶם שֹׁרֶשׁ פֹּרֶה״ — would it not have been sufficient to blend them and state them as one? Rather, he comes to give a reason why it is necessary to impose an oath upon the sons who are not yet born; therefore he speaks in the manner of negotiation: ״כִּי אַתֶּם יְדַעְתֶּם אֵת אֲשֶׁר יָשַׁבְנוּ בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ — and there we were slaves to Pharaoh, and HaShem redeemed us from there on condition that we accept upon ourselves the commandments of HaShem. But your sons who did not dwell in the land of Egypt and were not redeemed might argue: why should we accept upon ourselves the yoke of His commandments, for HaShem did not redeem us? Therefore I need to bring your sons into the covenant. And if you say: if so, why is there a need to impose an oath upon us — does He, blessed be He, not have a legal claim upon us, since on that condition He redeemed us? — to this He said: ״וְאֵת אֲשֶׁר עָבַרְנוּ בְּקֶרֶב הַגּוֹיִם וַתִּרְאוּ אֶת שִׁקּוּצֵיהֶם״ etc. — and behold, the bond and its cancellation are both in hand: for on the grounds that we dwelt in the land of Egypt there was no need to impose an oath upon us; but on the grounds that you saw the abominations of the nations among whom we passed, there is reason to fear ״פֶּן יֵשׁ בָּכֶם אִישׁ אוֹ אִשָּׁה״ etc.; therefore it is necessary to impose an oath upon them." "And if you say: if so, the difficulty returns to its place — for granted, those of us who saw the abominations of the nations needed to be sworn, for there is reason to fear lest we follow after them; but our sons who did not see those abominations, why would they go astray after them? — to this He said: ״פֶּן יֵשׁ בָּכֶם שֹׁרֶשׁ פֹּרֶה רֹאשׁ וְלַעֲנָה״. For in truth the sons who are born, on their own, have no cause to be drawn after idolatry, for they have never seen it; but there is reason to fear that from the root of the serpent a viper may emerge — for it is possible that one of you has harbored resentment in his heart, and the deeds of the fathers are inherited by the sons, for the nature of the son is drawn after the father like the branch that is drawn after the root. Therefore I need to impose an oath also upon the sons for this. And through this the two occurrences of ״פֶּן״ are well resolved: the first ״פֶּן״ refers to the fathers who saw the abominations of the nations — lest they follow after them; the second ״פֶּן״ refers to the sons who are born — for even though they did not see the abominations of the nations, nevertheless there is reason to fear that they will receive the nature of the root, that is, the fathers, who are a bitter root bearing wormwood and gall." "Or one may say that the entire passage, from ״כִּי אַתֶּם יְדַעְתֶּם״ until ״פֶּן יֵשׁ בָּכֶם שֹׁרֶשׁ״ etc., is all giving a reason concerning the fathers. For He says: since you dwelt in the land of Egypt and departed from there, and the gods of Egypt were unable to deliver Egypt, and afterward you passed through the midst of the nations whose lands HaShem did not grant you permission to possess — behold, through this you will come to error, saying that judgment was executed upon the gods of Egypt but not upon the gods of the nations through whom you passed, because your god is stronger than the gods of Egypt but not than the gods of the nations through whom we passed; and you will say that for this reason you could do nothing to those nations, for their god is with them to deliver them. This is what it says: ״וַתִּרְאוּ אֶת שִׁקּוּצֵיהֶם וְגוֹ׳ כֶּסֶף וְזָהָב אֲשֶׁר עִמָּהֶם״ — with the word ״עִמָּהֶם״ He sealed all their error, for they would say that their god is with them to deliver them. And had we not dwelt in the land of Egypt the error would not have been so strong, for perhaps the Holy One, blessed be He, does not take vengeance upon the idol-gods of the nations, and therefore He does not execute judgments upon them. But since He executed judgments upon the gods of Egypt and not upon the gods of the nations through whom we passed, from this you are close to coming to error, to say that the gods of the nations are with them to deliver them. And afterward He said concerning the sons: ״פֶּן יֵשׁ בָּכֶם שֹׁרֶשׁ פֹּרֶה״, in the manner explained above."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {0 "The abbreviation מהרי״א likely refers to Maharai, i.e., R. Yom-Tov Lipmann Heller's predecessor or a different commentator; resolved as transcribed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:15:26.591090940Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}